\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces CIFAR-10 comparison curves for FedAvg-OTA, FedAvgM-OTA, AdaGrad-OTA, and Adam-OTA. The loss panel uses a logarithmic $y$-axis because AdaGrad-OTA and Adam-OTA have very large but finite early-round transient test losses before recovering. This transient comes from the first-round numerator/denominator imbalance: with $v_0{=}0$, the adaptive denominator $v_t^{1/\alpha }{+}\varepsilon $ reduces to $\varepsilon \in \{10^{-4},10^{-8}\}$, so the first effective step is on the order of $\eta /\varepsilon $. After a few rounds, $v_t$ accumulates and the effective step normalizes.}}{16}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cifar-curves}{{4.1}{16}{CIFAR-10 comparison curves for FedAvg-OTA, FedAvgM-OTA, AdaGrad-OTA, and Adam-OTA. The loss panel uses a logarithmic $y$-axis because AdaGrad-OTA and Adam-OTA have very large but finite early-round transient test losses before recovering. This transient comes from the first-round numerator/denominator imbalance: with $v_0{=}0$, the adaptive denominator $v_t^{1/\alpha }{+}\varepsilon $ reduces to $\varepsilon \in \{10^{-4},10^{-8}\}$, so the first effective step is on the order of $\eta /\varepsilon $. After a few rounds, $v_t$ accumulates and the effective step normalizes}{figure.caption.9}{}}
